# ~/.zshenv — read by EVERY zsh, interactive or not, and before ~/.zshrc.
#
# Two kinds of thing belong here and nothing else:
#   1. Credentials a NON-interactive shell needs. Agent shells, MCP servers and
#      `zsh -c` never source ~/.zshrc, so anything they depend on has to live
#      here or it simply is not there.
#   2. Nothing that prints. A stray echo in this file corrupts every scp, rsync
#      and `ssh host command` on the machine.
#
# No secret literal is ever written into this file. Every value below is read
# from a 0600 file under ~/.config that a run_once script provisions from
# 1Password. That separation is the whole reason this file can be tracked in a
# public repo, and it must survive any future edit.

# --- Linear personal API key -------------------------------------------------
# Provisioned by run_once_after_20-linear-api-key.sh from op://agents/linear_api_key.
# Read from the file rather than shelling out to `op` here: .zshenv runs on every
# single zsh invocation, so a network round-trip per shell is not affordable.
_linear_key=~/.config/linear/gql-key
[[ -r $_linear_key ]] && export LINEAR_API_KEY="$(<$_linear_key)"
unset _linear_key

# --- Supabase connection string ----------------------------------------------
# Provisioned by run_once_before_21-supabase-pg-url.sh. This used to be rendered
# into ~/.zshrc by a chezmoi template, which broke once agent shells switched to
# a service-account token: a service account cannot see the Personal vault, so
# rendering silently produced an empty value and `chezmoi apply` from an agent
# shell rewrote ~/.zshrc without the variable. Reading a file at shell start
# instead means apply never touches the secret at all.
_supabase_url=~/.config/supabase/pg-url
[[ -r $_supabase_url ]] && export SUPABASE_PG_URL="$(<$_supabase_url)"
unset _supabase_url

# --- 1Password auth split (see: agent popup storm, 2026-08-26) ---------------
# Agent + headless shells authenticate with a service-account token: fully
# headless, so `op` never talks to the 1Password desktop app. That contact is
# what raised two prompts on *every* invocation under Supacode — a macOS
# "access data from other apps" TCC prompt (op reads 1Password's group
# container) and 1Password's own "allow zmx CLI access" (its client cache is
# keyed on the calling process tree, and Claude Code spawns a fresh one per
# command, so the cache never hit).
# Interactive shells deliberately keep biometrics: one unlock per terminal is
# cheap, and the token is a bearer secret we don't want backing a human session.
#
# The token file is NOT provisioned by chezmoi and never can be — it is the one
# credential that cannot bootstrap itself from 1Password. A new machine gets it
# by hand; see SETUP.md "Step 2b". Absence is silent and harmless here: `op`
# simply falls back to the desktop app, which is correct for a human terminal
# and merely unavailable for an agent one.
_op_token=~/.config/op/agent-token
if [[ -r $_op_token && ( -n $CLAUDECODE || ! -o interactive ) ]]; then
  export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN="$(<$_op_token)"
fi
unset _op_token
